1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,129
Welcome to School Computer Science,
Explained. This is Intro to Computer

2
00:00:04,129 --> 00:00:08,143
Science, Unit 5: Networks and the
Internet. Let's find out what really

3
00:00:08,143 --> 00:00:12,674
happens when you open a web page. After
every lesson, we'll work through a full

4
00:00:12,674 --> 00:00:16,917
example step by step, and we'll finish by
putting the whole unit together.

5
00:00:18,091 --> 00:00:22,632
Every time you send a message or load a
video, your device talks to other

6
00:00:22,632 --> 00:00:23,254
computers.

7
00:00:23,704 --> 00:00:26,584
To do that, they need to be connected.
That's a network.

8
00:00:27,734 --> 00:00:31,510
A network is two or more devices linked so
they can share data.

9
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:37,051
The devices are called nodes. Phones,
laptops, printers and servers can all be

10
00:00:37,051 --> 00:00:37,442
nodes.

11
00:00:38,592 --> 00:00:43,956
The links can be wired, like ethernet
cables and fiber optic lines, or wireless,

12
00:00:43,956 --> 00:00:46,571
like Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and cell signals.

13
00:00:47,021 --> 00:00:51,512
Wired links are usually faster and
steadier. Wireless links let you move

14
00:00:51,512 --> 00:00:51,949
around.

15
00:00:53,099 --> 00:00:58,489
A small network, like the one in your home
or school, is called a LAN, a local area

16
00:00:58,489 --> 00:00:59,008
network.

17
00:00:59,458 --> 00:01:04,984
Networks that cover cities or countries
are called WANs, wide area networks.

18
00:01:06,134 --> 00:01:10,832
Inside a home, a router connects your
devices to each other and to your internet

19
00:01:10,832 --> 00:01:11,360
provider.

20
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:16,916
The internet is a network of networks:
millions of LANs joined together around

21
00:01:16,916 --> 00:01:17,570
the world.

22
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:23,358
Networks are measured by bandwidth, how
much data they can send per second, in

23
00:01:23,358 --> 00:01:24,310
bits per second.

24
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:29,410
They're also measured by latency, how long
a single message takes to arrive.

25
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:36,521
Your turn. A school's network connects
every computer in one building. Is it a

26
00:01:36,521 --> 00:01:37,515
LAN or a WAN?

27
00:01:37,965 --> 00:01:39,160
Pause the video now.

28
00:01:39,610 --> 00:01:43,450
It covers one building, so it's a LAN, a
local area network.

29
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:46,886
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

30
00:01:48,036 --> 00:01:52,580
Let's look at four networks you use every
day, and describe each one correctly.

31
00:01:53,730 --> 00:01:59,297
The problem: for each network, decide
whether it's a LAN or a WAN, and whether

32
00:01:59,297 --> 00:02:01,581
its links are wired or wireless.

33
00:02:02,031 --> 00:02:06,436
Your home Wi-Fi, the school computer lab,
a phone's cell connection, and the

34
00:02:06,436 --> 00:02:07,364
internet itself.

35
00:02:08,514 --> 00:02:14,101
Use two questions. First: does it cover
one building, or a large area? One

36
00:02:14,101 --> 00:02:17,197
building is a LAN. A large area is a WAN.

37
00:02:17,647 --> 00:02:21,081
Second: does the data travel through
cables, or through the air?

38
00:02:22,231 --> 00:02:25,858
Home Wi-Fi: one home, through the air. A
wireless LAN.

39
00:02:26,308 --> 00:02:30,404
The school lab: one building, ethernet
cables. A wired LAN.

40
00:02:30,854 --> 00:02:35,483
The cell network: towers across a whole
region. A wireless WAN.

41
00:02:35,933 --> 00:02:41,011
The internet: the whole world, mostly
fiber cables. The biggest WAN of all.

42
00:02:42,161 --> 00:02:47,268
Every device in these networks is a node:
your phone, the lab computers, the routers

43
00:02:47,268 --> 00:02:48,241
and the servers.

44
00:02:48,691 --> 00:02:54,187
Tip: wired is usually faster and steadier,
wireless lets you move. Many networks mix

45
00:02:54,187 --> 00:02:54,515
both.

46
00:02:55,665 --> 00:03:01,106
Your turn. A hospital connects its
buildings in three different cities. Is

47
00:03:01,106 --> 00:03:02,577
that a LAN or a WAN?

48
00:03:03,027 --> 00:03:04,221
Pause the video now.

49
00:03:04,671 --> 00:03:07,530
A WAN. It covers a large area, not one
building.

50
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:09,452
Next, packets and routing.

51
00:03:10,602 --> 00:03:14,719
When you send a photo across the internet,
it doesn't travel in one piece.

52
00:03:15,169 --> 00:03:17,452
It's chopped into small chunks called
packets.

53
00:03:18,602 --> 00:03:22,079
Each packet holds a small piece of the
data, plus a header.

54
00:03:22,529 --> 00:03:27,090
The header is like the outside of an
envelope. It has the sender's address, the

55
00:03:27,090 --> 00:03:30,785
destination address, and a number that
says which piece this is.

56
00:03:31,935 --> 00:03:34,559
Packets travel through devices called
routers.

57
00:03:35,009 --> 00:03:39,423
Each router looks at a packet's
destination, and sends it one step closer,

58
00:03:39,423 --> 00:03:40,556
to the next router.

59
00:03:41,706 --> 00:03:44,458
Packets from the same file can take
different paths.

60
00:03:44,908 --> 00:03:50,123
If one route is busy or broken, routers
send packets another way. This makes the

61
00:03:50,123 --> 00:03:54,231
internet fault tolerant: it keeps working
even when parts fail.

62
00:03:55,381 --> 00:03:58,837
Because packets take different paths, they
can arrive out of order.

63
00:03:59,287 --> 00:04:04,341
The receiving computer uses the numbers in
the headers to put them back together. If

64
00:04:04,341 --> 00:04:06,988
a packet goes missing, it asks for it
again.

65
00:04:08,138 --> 00:04:13,270
Why not send one big piece? Small packets
share the network fairly, and if one gets

66
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:16,053
lost, only that small piece has to be
resent.

67
00:04:16,503 --> 00:04:18,529
This idea is called packet switching.

68
00:04:19,679 --> 00:04:24,640
Your turn. A file is split into packets
one, two, three and four. They arrive in

69
00:04:24,640 --> 00:04:28,298
the order three, one, four, two. What does
the receiver do?

70
00:04:28,748 --> 00:04:29,943
Pause the video now.

71
00:04:30,393 --> 00:04:34,873
It uses the packet numbers to put them
back in order: one, two, three, four.

72
00:04:35,323 --> 00:04:38,309
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

73
00:04:39,459 --> 00:04:43,875
Let's split a message into packets, send
them, and rebuild it on the other side.

74
00:04:45,025 --> 00:04:49,751
The problem: you send a six thousand byte
message. Each packet carries fifteen

75
00:04:49,751 --> 00:04:51,084
hundred bytes of data.

76
00:04:51,534 --> 00:04:55,631
How many packets are there, and what
happens if they arrive out of order, or

77
00:04:55,631 --> 00:04:56,547
one goes missing?

78
00:04:57,697 --> 00:05:00,726
Divide the size by the packet size to
count packets.

79
00:05:01,176 --> 00:05:05,391
Each header holds the source, the
destination, and a piece number, so the

80
00:05:05,391 --> 00:05:07,470
receiver can put them back in order.

81
00:05:08,620 --> 00:05:13,121
The packets take different routes, so they
arrive as piece two, then four, then one.

82
00:05:13,571 --> 00:05:17,859
Piece three hasn't arrived. The receiver
asks for it again, and it arrives.

83
00:05:18,309 --> 00:05:22,746
Now the receiver sorts them: one, two,
three, four. The message is complete.

84
00:05:23,896 --> 00:05:28,485
Four packets, rebuilt perfectly, even
though they arrived in a different order

85
00:05:28,485 --> 00:05:29,486
and one was lost.

86
00:05:29,936 --> 00:05:33,946
That's why the internet keeps working when
a route is busy or broken.

87
00:05:35,096 --> 00:05:40,141
Your turn. A three million byte photo is
sent in fifteen hundred byte packets. How

88
00:05:40,141 --> 00:05:41,432
many packets is that?

89
00:05:41,882 --> 00:05:43,077
Pause the video now.

90
00:05:43,527 --> 00:05:44,828
Two thousand packets.

91
00:05:45,278 --> 00:05:47,326
Next, IP addresses and DNS.

92
00:05:48,477 --> 00:05:53,277
Every packet needs a destination address.
On the internet, that's an IP address.

93
00:05:53,727 --> 00:05:55,476
And DNS is how we find it.

94
00:05:56,626 --> 00:06:00,872
An IP address is a number that identifies
a device on a network.

95
00:06:01,322 --> 00:06:07,244
The common version, IPv4, is four numbers
from zero to two fifty five, separated by

96
00:06:07,244 --> 00:06:11,668
dots, like one ninety two dot one sixty
eight dot one dot ten.

97
00:06:12,818 --> 00:06:17,725
IPv4 has thirty two bits, so there are
about four point three billion addresses.

98
00:06:18,175 --> 00:06:22,887
That's not enough for every device on
Earth. So the newer IPv6 uses one hundred

99
00:06:22,887 --> 00:06:26,346
twenty eight bits, which is far more than
we'll ever need.

100
00:06:27,496 --> 00:06:32,018
People don't remember numbers well. We
type names, like example dot com.

101
00:06:32,468 --> 00:06:37,595
DNS, the domain name system, is the
internet's phone book. It turns a name

102
00:06:37,595 --> 00:06:38,911
into an IP address.

103
00:06:40,061 --> 00:06:45,096
When you type a web address, your computer
asks a DNS server for the IP address.

104
00:06:45,546 --> 00:06:50,485
The server answers, and your computer
sends its packets to that address. Answers

105
00:06:50,485 --> 00:06:53,695
are saved for a while, so the next lookup
is faster.

106
00:06:54,845 --> 00:06:59,410
Your computer also has an IP address, so
replies know where to come back.

107
00:06:59,860 --> 00:07:03,828
At home, your router hands out addresses
to each device that joins.

108
00:07:04,978 --> 00:07:10,141
Your turn. Is two sixty dot one dot one
dot one a valid IPv4 address?

109
00:07:10,591 --> 00:07:11,786
Pause the video now.

110
00:07:12,236 --> 00:07:17,398
No. Each part must be from zero to two
fifty five, and two sixty is too big.

111
00:07:17,848 --> 00:07:20,835
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

112
00:07:21,985 --> 00:07:27,122
Let's follow exactly what happens when you
type a website name, before a single page

113
00:07:27,122 --> 00:07:27,489
loads.

114
00:07:28,639 --> 00:07:33,829
The problem: you type games dot example
dot com. Your computer needs its IP

115
00:07:33,829 --> 00:07:36,874
address. Who does it ask, and in what
order?

116
00:07:38,024 --> 00:07:43,104
Here's the path of a DNS lookup. First
check the device's own memory, called the

117
00:07:43,104 --> 00:07:43,485
cache.

118
00:07:43,935 --> 00:07:49,181
If it's not there, ask a resolver, usually
run by your internet provider. The

119
00:07:49,181 --> 00:07:54,359
resolver asks the root, then the dot com
servers, then example dot com's own

120
00:07:54,359 --> 00:07:54,836
server.

121
00:07:55,986 --> 00:07:59,314
Your cache doesn't know it. The resolver
doesn't know it either.

122
00:07:59,764 --> 00:08:04,932
The root server says: ask the dot com
servers. The dot com server says: ask

123
00:08:04,932 --> 00:08:06,655
example dot com's server.

124
00:08:07,105 --> 00:08:12,622
That server answers: two oh three dot zero
dot one thirteen dot seven. The resolver

125
00:08:12,622 --> 00:08:15,148
remembers it, and tells your computer.

126
00:08:16,298 --> 00:08:21,713
Your browser can now send packets to two
oh three dot zero dot one thirteen dot

127
00:08:21,713 --> 00:08:24,660
seven. It all takes a fraction of a
second.

128
00:08:25,110 --> 00:08:29,121
Next time, the cache has the answer, so
the lookup is almost instant.

129
00:08:30,271 --> 00:08:35,711
Your turn. Is two fifty six dot ten dot
one dot one a valid IPv4 address?

130
00:08:36,161 --> 00:08:37,356
Pause the video now.

131
00:08:37,806 --> 00:08:41,048
No. Each part must be from zero to two
fifty five.

132
00:08:41,498 --> 00:08:43,312
Next, the web and HTTP.

133
00:08:44,461 --> 00:08:46,850
The internet and the web are not the same
thing.

134
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:52,481
The internet is the network. The web is
one service that runs on it: pages and

135
00:08:52,481 --> 00:08:54,340
links you open in a browser.

136
00:08:55,490 --> 00:08:58,456
A web address is called a URL. It has
parts.

137
00:08:58,906 --> 00:09:05,695
https is the protocol. example dot com is
the domain. And slash games is the path to

138
00:09:05,695 --> 00:09:07,311
a page on that site.

139
00:09:08,461 --> 00:09:10,765
The web uses a client and server model.

140
00:09:11,215 --> 00:09:15,925
Your browser is the client. It sends a
request. A web server sends back a

141
00:09:15,925 --> 00:09:16,506
response.

142
00:09:17,656 --> 00:09:23,010
The language of that conversation is HTTP,
the hypertext transfer protocol.

143
00:09:23,460 --> 00:09:29,158
A request like GET slash games asks for a
page. The response starts with a status

144
00:09:29,158 --> 00:09:33,167
code. Two hundred means OK. Four oh four
means not found.

145
00:09:34,317 --> 00:09:38,221
The response holds HTML, the code that
describes the page.

146
00:09:38,671 --> 00:09:44,054
The browser reads the HTML, fetches the
images and styles it mentions, and draws

147
00:09:44,054 --> 00:09:45,668
the page on your screen.

148
00:09:46,818 --> 00:09:49,848
HTTPS is HTTP with encryption.

149
00:09:50,298 --> 00:09:55,325
The data is scrambled between your browser
and the server, so others on the network

150
00:09:55,325 --> 00:09:58,596
can't read it. Look for it whenever you
log in or pay.

151
00:09:59,746 --> 00:10:04,450
Your turn. You request a page, and the
server answers with status code four oh

152
00:10:04,450 --> 00:10:05,656
four. What happened?

153
00:10:06,106 --> 00:10:07,300
Pause the video now.

154
00:10:07,750 --> 00:10:12,806
Four oh four means not found. The server
is working, but that page doesn't exist.

155
00:10:13,256 --> 00:10:16,243
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

156
00:10:17,393 --> 00:10:22,338
Let's take apart a real-looking URL, and
read the server's answers to four

157
00:10:22,338 --> 00:10:22,940
requests.

158
00:10:24,090 --> 00:10:31,189
The problem: split this URL into its
parts: https, colon slash slash, www dot

159
00:10:31,189 --> 00:10:34,970
example dot com, slash games slash chess.

160
00:10:35,420 --> 00:10:37,638
Then decide what four status codes mean.

161
00:10:38,788 --> 00:10:41,412
Here are the parts, and the request the
browser sends.

162
00:10:41,862 --> 00:10:46,775
The server's response starts with a status
code. Two hundreds mean success, three

163
00:10:46,775 --> 00:10:51,626
hundreds mean moved, four hundreds mean
your mistake, and five hundreds mean the

164
00:10:51,626 --> 00:10:52,657
server's mistake.

165
00:10:53,807 --> 00:10:55,791
Two hundred: OK. Here's the page.

166
00:10:56,241 --> 00:11:00,956
Three oh one: the page moved permanently.
The browser follows the new address.

167
00:11:01,406 --> 00:11:04,563
Four oh four: not found. Maybe you typed
the path wrong.

168
00:11:05,013 --> 00:11:08,746
Five hundred: the server itself had an
error. Try again later.

169
00:11:09,896 --> 00:11:12,947
You can now read any URL and any status
code.

170
00:11:13,397 --> 00:11:19,309
Tip: the s in https means the connection
is encrypted. Look for it before typing

171
00:11:19,309 --> 00:11:20,565
anything private.

172
00:11:21,715 --> 00:11:27,921
Your turn. In https colon slash slash shop
dot example dot org slash cart, what is

173
00:11:27,921 --> 00:11:30,419
the domain, and what is the path?

174
00:11:30,869 --> 00:11:32,064
Pause the video now.

175
00:11:32,514 --> 00:11:36,802
The domain is shop dot example dot org.
The path is slash cart.

176
00:11:37,252 --> 00:11:38,276
Next, the cloud.

177
00:11:39,426 --> 00:11:42,861
Your photos are in the cloud. Your game
saves are in the cloud.

178
00:11:43,311 --> 00:11:44,761
But where is the cloud, really?

179
00:11:45,911 --> 00:11:48,450
The cloud is just other people's
computers.

180
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:53,380
They're servers in huge buildings called
data centers, owned by companies, and

181
00:11:53,380 --> 00:11:54,873
reached over the internet.

182
00:11:56,023 --> 00:12:01,114
A data center holds thousands of servers
in racks, with powerful cooling, backup

183
00:12:01,114 --> 00:12:03,341
power, and very fast network links.

184
00:12:03,791 --> 00:12:06,863
Big companies run data centers all over
the world.

185
00:12:08,013 --> 00:12:10,061
Cloud services come in a few kinds.

186
00:12:10,511 --> 00:12:15,404
Cloud storage keeps your files, like a
photo backup. Cloud apps run in your

187
00:12:15,404 --> 00:12:20,492
browser, like online documents. And
developers can rent computing power to run

188
00:12:20,492 --> 00:12:21,732
their own programs.

189
00:12:22,882 --> 00:12:28,164
The cloud has big benefits. You can reach
your files from any device. Data is copied

190
00:12:28,164 --> 00:12:33,132
to several places, so one broken drive
doesn't lose it. And you can use as much

191
00:12:33,132 --> 00:12:34,893
computing power as you need.

192
00:12:35,343 --> 00:12:39,457
But there are tradeoffs. You need an
internet connection, and you're trusting a

193
00:12:39,457 --> 00:12:40,655
company with your data.

194
00:12:41,805 --> 00:12:46,408
So when an app syncs your work between
your phone and your laptop, it's sending

195
00:12:46,408 --> 00:12:50,487
data to a server in a data center, and
back down to your other device.

196
00:12:51,637 --> 00:12:56,124
Your turn. Your laptop breaks, but your
photos are safe on your new phone. Where

197
00:12:56,124 --> 00:12:57,077
were they stored?

198
00:12:57,527 --> 00:12:58,722
Pause the video now.

199
00:12:59,172 --> 00:13:03,033
In the cloud: on servers in a data center,
reached over the internet.

200
00:13:03,483 --> 00:13:06,470
Next, let's work through a full example,
step by step.

201
00:13:07,620 --> 00:13:12,113
Let's work out if a photo backup fits in
the cloud, and how long it takes to

202
00:13:12,113 --> 00:13:12,527
upload.

203
00:13:13,677 --> 00:13:17,773
The problem: you have two thousand photos,
about three megabytes each.

204
00:13:18,223 --> 00:13:23,040
Does it fit in a free five gigabyte plan?
And how long does it take to upload at

205
00:13:23,040 --> 00:13:24,665
twenty megabits per second?

206
00:13:25,815 --> 00:13:30,850
Total size: photos times size, then divide
by a thousand to get gigabytes.

207
00:13:31,300 --> 00:13:36,205
For upload time, turn gigabytes into
megabits: times a thousand, then times

208
00:13:36,205 --> 00:13:38,297
eight. Then divide by the speed.

209
00:13:39,447 --> 00:13:44,274
Six thousand megabytes is six gigabytes.
That's more than five, so the free plan

210
00:13:44,274 --> 00:13:45,058
isn't enough.

211
00:13:45,508 --> 00:13:50,536
Forty eight thousand megabits at twenty
per second is two thousand four hundred

212
00:13:50,536 --> 00:13:53,081
seconds. Divide by sixty: forty minutes.

213
00:13:54,231 --> 00:13:58,071
You'd need a bigger plan, and the first
backup takes about forty minutes.

214
00:13:58,521 --> 00:14:03,769
Common mistake: forgetting that internet
speeds are in bits, and file sizes are in

215
00:14:03,769 --> 00:14:05,689
bytes. One byte is eight bits.

216
00:14:06,839 --> 00:14:11,989
Your turn. How long does a one gigabyte
video take to upload at forty megabits per

217
00:14:11,989 --> 00:14:12,429
second?

218
00:14:12,879 --> 00:14:14,073
Pause the video now.

219
00:14:14,523 --> 00:14:19,393
One gigabyte is eight thousand megabits.
Divided by forty, that's two hundred

220
00:14:19,393 --> 00:14:19,899
seconds.

221
00:14:20,349 --> 00:14:22,333
Next, let's put the whole unit together.

222
00:14:23,483 --> 00:14:28,006
You've learned networks, packets, IP
addresses, DNS, the web and the cloud.

223
00:14:28,456 --> 00:14:33,051
Now let's follow one click, from your
laptop to a data center and back, using

224
00:14:33,051 --> 00:14:33,768
all of them.

225
00:14:34,918 --> 00:14:41,387
Here's the plan. You're on the school
Wi-Fi, and you open https colon slash

226
00:14:41,387 --> 00:14:44,923
slash www dot example dot com slash news.

227
00:14:45,373 --> 00:14:46,696
We'll follow every step.

228
00:14:47,846 --> 00:14:51,899
Step one: your laptop is a node on the
school's wireless LAN.

229
00:14:52,349 --> 00:14:57,042
The school router connects that LAN to the
internet, the biggest WAN.

230
00:14:58,192 --> 00:15:04,030
Step two: the browser needs an IP address.
The cache doesn't have it, so DNS asks the

231
00:15:04,030 --> 00:15:08,838
resolver, the root, the dot com servers,
and example dot com's server.

232
00:15:09,288 --> 00:15:13,277
The answer: two oh three dot zero dot one
thirteen dot seven.

233
00:15:14,427 --> 00:15:18,736
Step three: the browser writes an HTTP
request: GET slash news.

234
00:15:19,186 --> 00:15:22,813
It's https, so the request is encrypted on
the way.

235
00:15:23,963 --> 00:15:28,684
Step four: the request is split into
packets, each with a header holding the

236
00:15:28,684 --> 00:15:29,616
destination IP.

237
00:15:30,066 --> 00:15:34,013
Routers pass them along, one hop at a
time, maybe on different paths.

238
00:15:35,163 --> 00:15:40,593
Step five: a server in a data center, part
of the cloud, answers: two hundred, OK,

239
00:15:40,593 --> 00:15:41,520
with the page.

240
00:15:41,970 --> 00:15:46,860
The page comes back as thirty packets.
Your laptop sorts them, and the news page

241
00:15:46,860 --> 00:15:48,754
appears. All in under a second.

242
00:15:49,904 --> 00:15:54,710
Your turn. You click a link and get a four
oh four. Did DNS work? Did the server

243
00:15:54,710 --> 00:15:55,131
answer?

244
00:15:55,581 --> 00:15:56,776
Pause the video now.

245
00:15:57,226 --> 00:16:01,450
Yes to both. The server answered, but the
page at that path doesn't exist.

246
00:16:01,900 --> 00:16:06,102
That's the end of Unit 5. Next, Unit 6:
staying safe online.

